<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">Aruba</h1><p class="st-meta">1986 CE – present · capital Oranjestad</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Island off Venezuela that left the Netherlands Antilles in 1986 to become a separate country within the Kingdom of the Netherlands.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Arubans had long resented domination by <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/curacao">Curaçao</a> within the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/colony-of-curacao">Colony of Curaçao</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/netherlands-antilles">Netherlands Antilles</a>. Led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/betico-croes">Gilberto 'Betico' Croes</a> and his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-electoral-movement-aruba">People's Electoral Movement</a>, they won a referendum for separation in 1977, and on 1 January 1986 Aruba gained 'status aparte' as an autonomous country in the kingdom, with independence planned for 1996.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Aruba has its own parliament, prime minister, constitution and currency, the florin, while the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> manages defence and foreign affairs and a governor represents the monarch. The closure of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lago-oil-and-transport-company">Lago</a> oil refinery in 1985 forced a rapid turn to tourism, which now dominates. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papiamento">Papiamento</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> are official languages. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henny-eman">Henny Eman</a> became the first prime minister, followed by leaders such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nelson-oduber">Nelson Oduber</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/evelyn-wever-croes">Evelyn Wever-Croes</a>.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p>In 1990 Aruba asked to postpone independence, and in 1995 the plan was dropped, leaving it a permanent country within the kingdom. It remains among the most prosperous <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> islands, though heavily dependent on American tourism.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/aruba.jpg" alt="Aruba" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Original: Whitney Smith Vector: Wester · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Aruba.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1986 CE – present</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Oranjestad</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Henny Eman, Nelson Oduber, Evelyn Wever-Croes</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Papiamento and Dutch</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Aruba</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Country of Aruba</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=2006&amp;focus=aruba" aria-label="Aruba on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2757 1711 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Aruba lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M3077 1967L5110 626M3077 1967L3106 1982"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5110 626h0M3106 1982h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3077 1967h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3077 1967h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Oranjestad. <a href="/atlas?year=2006&amp;focus=aruba">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
